This extension adds Lobotomy Corporation modding support to Vortex Mod Manager.
[summary]BaseMod automatically installed.[/summary]

[b]✅ Supported Versions:[/b]
[list]
[*]Steam[/*]
[*]Other versions may need to select the installation location manually.[/*]
[/list]
[b]🛠️ Mod Installation Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*]BaseMod Loader installs to the "LobotomyCorp_Data\Managed" folder, recognised by a "LobotomyBaseModLib.dll" file.[/*]
[*]Root Folder mods with a "LobotomyCorp_Data" folder install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]BaseMod mods install to the "LobotomyCorp_Data\BaseMods" folder.[/*]
[*]Any other mod not described above installs to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Archives with a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with ModuleConfig.xml) are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead.[/*]
[/list]
[b]📋 Usage Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*]Use the Load Order page to set the order mods load in, and to enable or disable them.[/*]
[*]The load order is written to "BaseModList_v2.xml" in the mods folder. That is the same file the Lobotomy Mod Manager uses, so both tools show the same order and the same enabled mods.[/*]
[*]Mod folders added by hand, without an entry in that file, load after everything else and are always enabled. They appear at the bottom of the Load Order page.[/*]
[*]Unchecking a mod leaves its files installed and tells the loader to skip it.[/*]
[*]Installing the loader replaces the game's "Assembly-CSharp.dll". Vortex backs the original up and restores it when you purge, so you can return the game to an unmodded state at any time.[/*]
[*]The Lobotomy Mod Manager is downloaded once from its Nexus Mods page so the page gets the download credit. That copy is not installed and is not needed - the loader itself is installed from its newer official release.[/*]
[*]You can open several useful files/folders/URLs using the buttons within the folder icon on the Mods toolbar.[/*]
[*][b][color=#ffff00]Automatically installs [url=https://github.com/LobotomyBaseMod/LMM]BaseMod[/url], identified by a "LobotomyBaseModLib.dll" file.[/color][/b][/*]
[*]Installs dll plugins for BaseMod to the "LobotomyCorp_Data/BaseMods" folder. [b][color=#00ffff]Load Order support is included for these mods![/color][/b][/*]
[*]The load order is written to "BaseModList_v2.xml" in the mods folder.[/*]
[*]The Lobotomy Mod Manager is downloaded once from its Nexus Mods page so the page gets the download credit and Vortex Health Check won't flag it as missing. That copy is not installed and is not needed - the loader itself is installed from its newer official GitHub releases page.[/*]
[/list]

[b]📝 Notes for Mod Authors:[/b]
[spoiler]
Packaging rules for Lobotomy Corporation mods, so Vortex installs them to the right place.

Vortex decides what a mod is by looking at the files and folders inside the archive. It tries each installer in order and the first one that matches wins, so archive layout is what determines where your mod ends up.

========================================================================

BaseMod Loader:

Recognised when the archive contains a "LobotomyBaseModLib.dll" file.

  Installs to: "LobotomyCorp_Data\Managed"

========================================================================

Root / Game Folder Mods:

For mods laid out the same way the files appear inside the game folder. Vortex copies the matched folder and everything under it straight into the game.

[code]
MyRootMod.zip
└── LobotomyCorp_Data\
    └── ... files in their real relative locations
[/code]

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by a folder named "LobotomyCorp_Data" in the archive.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Zipping the folder that CONTAINS the game folders, instead of the game folders themselves, adds an extra level and misplaces every file.

========================================================================

BaseMod:

Handled by the "testMod" installer. Inspect the extension source for the exact archive layout it expects.

  Installs to: "LobotomyCorp_Data\BaseMods"

========================================================================

Fallback Installer:

The catch-all. Any archive that matched none of the installers above lands here and is copied across unchanged.

[b][color=#ffff00]NOTE: Landing in the fallback installer is a signal your archive layout needs fixing.[/color][/b]

  Requirements:
    - Reaching this installer usually means the archive was not laid out in a way Vortex recognised.
    - Vortex shows the user a notification when a mod installs through the fallback.

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - If your mod lands here unintentionally, re-check the layouts above - users will see a fallback warning and may report the mod as broken.

========================================================================

Rules That Apply To Every Mod Type:

    - Archives that contain a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with "ModuleConfig.xml") are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead, and none of the rules above apply.
    - Folder and file name matching is case-insensitive.
    - Extra wrapper folders around a recognised folder are generally fine; the installer searches at any depth.
[/spoiler]

